      "content": "Hashing Techniques"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "In the last lesson, one of the hash functions we used was H(x) = x mod 5."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "This function uses modulus (remainder when divided by a number) to compute a key's hash value, a technique known as modular hashing."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Modular hashing ensures that the hash values are distributed within a fixed range. For example,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Suppose we have this hash function H(x) = x mod 10. Let's examine its hash table for keys 1005, 12, 7, 99999, 4556, and 7838."
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "Hash Table",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "As you can see, the hash values for this function will be in a fixed range, 0 to 9, no matter how large the number is. This helps to utilize the hash table space efficiently."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "How does it work?"
